> API and interpretation

The static frequency model and message length are out-of-band inputs; their
transmission cost is not included. Empty messages still carry a four-byte flush.
A one-symbol model can represent arbitrarily many repetitions with that framing
plus an external length, so this is not a self-delimiting code. The decoder now
rejects missing bytes and out-of-model quotients; this detects some malformed
streams, not all corruption, and supplies no integrity/authentication guarantee.
Histories contain actual subdivisions and byte shifts. Explicit caps bound
symbols, alphabet, complete byte streams and retained records independently.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Martin, Range encoding: an algorithm for removing redundancy from a digitised message (1979)|https://sachingarg.com/compression/entropy_coding/range_coder.pdf]

